WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.659
A Go REST API is shaped by more than route
code.

2
00:00:02.739 --> 00:00:06.431
Dependencies, configuration, build
settings, tests, deployment choices,

3
00:00:06.511 --> 00:00:08.613
and release habits all influence security.

4
00:00:08.693 --> 00:00:10.568
Go modules make dependencies visible,

5
00:00:10.648 --> 00:00:14.056
but visible dependencies still need
ownership, review, update paths,

6
00:00:14.136 --> 00:00:15.159
and a reason to exist.

7
00:00:15.239 --> 00:00:17.909
go.mod and go.sum changes should be
reviewed with care.

8
00:00:17.989 --> 00:00:21.000
Teams should understand direct and
transitive dependencies,

9
00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:23.693
abandoned packages, version pins, module
checksums,

10
00:00:23.773 --> 00:00:25.761
vulnerability notices where available,

11
00:00:25.841 --> 00:00:29.477
and the process for replacing a package
that no longer fits the risk profile.

12
00:00:29.557 --> 00:00:32.625
Fewer unnecessary dependencies often means
less code to trust.

13
00:00:32.705 --> 00:00:35.545
Configuration should be explicit and
environment-aware.

14
00:00:35.625 --> 00:00:38.182
Development behavior, debug output, CORS
settings,

15
00:00:38.262 --> 00:00:41.898
TLS termination assumptions, service
credentials, runtime permissions,

16
00:00:41.978 --> 00:00:44.932
and feature flags should not drift
silently into production.

17
00:00:45.012 --> 00:00:47.454
Required settings should be validated at
startup,

18
00:00:47.534 --> 00:00:51.568
and sensitive values should come from
controlled configuration rather than source

19
00:00:51.648 --> 00:00:52.557
code or broad logs.

20
00:00:52.637 --> 00:00:56.613
Testing should cover security-relevant
behavior, not only successful requests.

21
00:00:56.693 --> 00:00:59.420
Handler tests, validation tests,
authorization tests,

22
00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:02.511
tenant-boundary tests, error-path tests,
timeout behavior,

23
00:01:02.591 --> 00:01:05.943
and configuration checks all help keep
assumptions from regressing.

24
00:01:06.023 --> 00:01:09.886
Negative tests are especially useful
because they prove the API rejects cases it

25
00:01:09.966 --> 00:01:10.704
should reject.

26
00:01:10.784 --> 00:01:12.886
Secure delivery is repeatable governance.

27
00:01:12.966 --> 00:01:16.545
Static analysis, linting, CI checks,
release notes, rollback readiness,

28
00:01:16.625 --> 00:01:20.716
patch workflows, and documented security
changes help teams ship without relying on

29
00:01:20.796 --> 00:01:21.193
memory.

30
00:01:21.273 --> 00:01:24.739
A maintainable API has a clear path for
routine fixes and urgent updates.

31
00:01:24.819 --> 00:01:28.454
The best delivery process makes important
risk visible before deployment.

32
00:01:28.534 --> 00:01:32.341
A dependency change, a new route, a new
tenant rule, a new environment variable,

33
00:01:32.421 --> 00:01:35.829
or a changed timeout should leave evidence
in review and release notes.

34
00:01:35.909 --> 00:01:40.056
That evidence helps operations understand
what changed and helps future maintainers

35
00:01:40.136 --> 00:01:44.340
patch confidently later, especially during
urgent maintenance or compliance review.
